    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7896bc1a_9255a6e2",
        "filename": "zephyr/drivers/cros_system/cros_system_it8xxx2.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 100,
      "author": {
        "id": 1273969
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-04-26T11:13:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec/+/refs/heads/main/common/keyboard_scan.c#749\nIn this example, when the system reboots, the chip can get power-on and reset-pin reset causes simultaneously. If we want the condition of reset source to be successfully matched with the reset-pin cause, the VCC1_RST_PIN must be reported.\n\nBut with such a change, we can only get the power-on reset cause instead of having all the reset causes simultaneously. That\u0027s why we need to store system_flags here.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 93,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 100,
        "endChar": 2
      },
      "revId": "f3d0ca3300e26eccd67f34dadadf48737c836c48",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ac96f970_04a3d78a",
        "filename": "zephyr/drivers/cros_system/cros_system_it8xxx2.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 100,
      "author": {
        "id": 1361259
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-04-27T00:16:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s true that the hardware might report multiple causes, but no other driver attempts to pass all of them through so I don\u0027t think this one should either.\n\nThe software tends to assume there is only one proximate cause of a reset, so I\u0027ve made this do that and prefer to report only the most fundamental of the reset causes the hardware reports.",
      "parentUuid": "7896bc1a_9255a6e2",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 93,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 100,
        "endChar": 2
      },
      "revId": "f3d0ca3300e26eccd67f34dadadf48737c836c48",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2879d050_dc7684af",
        "filename": "zephyr/drivers/cros_system/cros_system_it8xxx2.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 100,
      "author": {
        "id": 1153134
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-04-27T08:57:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We can not clearly distinguish the reset causes of reset-pin and power-on from IT81302 while reset the EC (power lost or toggle reset pin). Please see this issue:\nhttps://partnerissuetracker.corp.google.com/issues/179523020\n\nIf we want to report only the most fundamental of the reset causes, I would have the IT8XXX2_GCTRL_LRSIWR flag as the first condition to check. Because the the flag will be also set to 1 while power the EC on.\nSince reset pin is also able to clear write-protect state, we can change flash driver to check for the EC_RESET_FLAG_RESET_PIN flag instead of the EC_RESET_FLAG_POWER_ON flag:\nhttps://chromium.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec/+/refs/heads/main/zephyr/drivers/cros_flash/cros_flash_it8xxx2.c#147\n\nAnd one more thing we need to check it up.\nHow can we modify tc_state_init() function?\nWe no longer get the \"power-on\" reset cause.",
      "parentUuid": "ac96f970_04a3d78a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 93,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 100,
        "endChar": 2
      },
      "revId": "f3d0ca3300e26eccd67f34dadadf48737c836c48",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "81f4e18f_b7398efb",
        "filename": "zephyr/drivers/cros_system/cros_system_it8xxx2.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 100,
      "author": {
        "id": 1361259
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-04-28T04:55:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If I\u0027m understanding correctly, that means a power-on reset sets both LRSIWR to 1 and LRS to 0 or 1 (because #WRST is pulled up to VSTBY), and #WRST will do the same?\n\nIn that case I think it\u0027s most conservative to treat either of those resets (since they can\u0027t be differentiated) as power-on, as I have it doing right now. We could also remove the test of LRSIWR because we know that branch will never be taken (or go the other way, and remove the support for returning POWERUP). Is that right?",
      "parentUuid": "2879d050_dc7684af",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 93,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 100,
        "endChar": 2
      },
      "revId": "f3d0ca3300e26eccd67f34dadadf48737c836c48",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "372cc398_7813d9ba",
